1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Why didn't it use my index? Selectivity: what fraction of the rows does the query need?

2
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:12,100
A table lives on disk in pages. Reading a page costs work, whether you need 1 row from it or all 5.

3
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:14,100
4 of 200 rows match  →  selectivity = 2%

4
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:18,600
Index path: look up matches in the index, then jump to each page that has one.

5
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:23,100
Seq Scan: read every page once, in order, and test each row.

6
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:29,000
4 pages vs 40: the index wins easily.

7
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:31,000
160 of 200 rows match  →  selectivity = 80%

8
00:00:31,500 --> 00:00:36,000
Index path: look up matches in the index, then jump to each page that has one.

9
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:40,500
Seq Scan: read every page once, in order, and test each row.

10
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:44,400
Every page has a match, so the index can't skip any. Reading them straight through is cheaper.

11
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:54,000
There is no magic cutoff. The planner ESTIMATES the cost of each path and picks the cheaper one.

12
00:00:56,600 --> 00:01:06,600
Seq Scan ≠ "the index is missing": Selectivity = matching rows ÷ total rows. Few rows needed (low %): an index can skip most pages. Most rows needed (high %): just read every page once. Small tables: a scan is cheap anyway. The index may not match the query's column.

